true,"_score_rev_volatility":95.6,"_gate_margin_vs_hist":0.01962795626955123,"_gp_margin_vs_hist":true,"_score_margin_vs_hist":75.5,"_gate_piotroski":4,"_gp_piotroski":false,"_score_piotroski":44.4,"_gate_spread":-0.013255538492832813,"_gp_spread":false,"_score_spread":0.0,"_gate_roic_consistency":1.525532829157388,"_gp_roic_consistency":false,"_score_roic_consistency":0.0,"_gate_incr_roic":0.0219876136354199,"_gp_incr_roic":false,"_score_incr_roic":24.0,"_gate_margin_advantage":-0.10410417309294885,"_gp_margin_advantage":false,"_score_margin_advantage":0.0,"_gate_pool_share":-0.05189527205051303,"_gp_pool_share":false,"_score_pool_share":19.2,"_gate_rev_durability":null,"_gp_rev_durability":null,"_score_rev_durability":0.0,"_gate_fcf_durability":-0.08703532596363928,"_gp_fcf_durability":false,"_score_fcf_durability":0.0,"_gate_margins":null,"_gp_margins":null,"_score_margins":0.0,"_gate_fund_growth":0,"_gp_fund_growth":false,"_score_fund_growth":0.0,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.034104354712999545,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":true,"_score_shrhldr_yield":49.0,"_gate_share_shrink":-0.06524875952055686,"_gp_share_shrink":true,"_score_share_shrink":100.0,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.006370504002706055,"_gp_sbc_dilution":true,"_score_sbc_dilution":89.4,"_gate_insider_own":0.00765,"_gp_insider_own":false,"_score_insider_own":5.1,"_gate_insider_buying":0.176,"_gp_insider_buying":false,"_score_insider_buying":17.6,"pf":[],"description_full":"Vistra Corp., together with its subsidiaries, operates as an integrated retail electricity and power generation company in the United States. The company operates through five segments: Retail, Texas, East, West, and Asset Closure. The company retails electricity and natural gas to residential, commercial, and industrial customers across states in the United States and the District of Columbia. It is also involved in electricity generation, wholesale energy purchases and sales, commodity risk management, fuel procurement, and fuel logistics management activities. In addition, the company engages in decommissioning and reclamation of retired generation facilities, including mines, and battery removal and remediation activities. It serves approximately 5 million customers with a generation capacity of approximately 44,000 megawatts with a portfolio of natural gas, nuclear, coal, solar, and battery energy storage facilities. The company was formerly known as Vistra Energy Corp. and changed its name to Vistra Corp. in July 2020. Vistra Corp. was founded in 1882 and is based in Irving, Texas.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Kristopher E. Moldovan | Executive VP & CFO | Age 53 | Compensation: $1.5M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 6K people. Revenue per employee of $2.8M ranks in the top quartile among Utilities peers, with revenue growing 7%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $206K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $18K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 1\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags low compensation governance risk (2/10).","financial_summary":["Vistra Corp. is a large-cap utilities business with a $39.1B market cap on $17.7B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +9% over the past three years, a respectable pace for a business of this size.","Returns on capital run at 8% against a 9% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests.","The business throws off $1.3B in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 7 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 4.31\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, debt is heavy at 4.3\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and leverage like that is what turns a bad year into a permanent loss, and thin 1.6\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield of 3.4% reflects a genuine commitment to returning capital rather than empire-building, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (1.1) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Low reinvestment-driven growth suggests the compounding engine has stalled","sev":"amber","cat":"growth"},{"text":"Operating margins softening -1.0pp YoY \u2014 early warning on the moat","sev":"amber","cat":"trend"},{"text":"Operating margins 10% below sector median \u2014 a cost disadvantage the moat cannot hide","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Price sits 18% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left","sev":"amber","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.17503891915837297},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Piotroski score 4/9 \u2014 below the median for a healthy business","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Insiders are net sellers (82% sell ratio, net $24.7M) \u2014 the people closest to the business are reducing their personal stake","sev":"amber","cat":"insider"},{"text":"Wildfire and climate liability \u2014 extreme weather events create catastrophic, uninsurable losses that can push even investment-grade utilities toward restructuring.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Trading near the bottom of its 52-week range \u2014 the market is offering a discount","Valued at a 34% discount to sector median EV/EBITDA","Healthy payout ratio leaves room for both dividend growth and reinvestment","Growing faster than most Utilities peers \u2014 taking share inside the profit pool","Utilities sector showing strong relative momentum, outperforming the market by 9% over 3 months","Utilities tends to hold up well as investors rotate into defensive names"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"How Are Power Demand Trends Lifting Results at Vistra (NYSE:VST) Now?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/us/stocks/energy/how-are-power-demand-trends-lifting-results-at-vistra-nysevst-now","date":"2026-11-20","timestamp":1795160400.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":-0.25,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":["earnings"]},{"title":"How Is Power Demand Lifting Quarterly Results at Vistra (NYSE:VST)?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/us/stocks/energy/how-is-power-demand-lifting-quarterly-results-at-vistra-nysevst","date":"2026-11-19","timestamp":1795067520.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":-0.25,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":["earnings"]},{"title":"Why Is Vistra (NYSE:VST) Facing Pressure Despite Strong Results?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/us/stocks/utility/why-is-vistra-nysevst-facing-pressure-despite-strong-results","date":"2026-11-19","timestamp":1795067280.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":-0.847,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":["earnings"]},{"title":"Vistra (VST) Stock Could Be Undervalued On Its 7x Five Year Run","source":"simplywall.st","link":"https://simplywall.st/stocks/us/utilities/nyse-vst/vistra/news/vistra-vst-stock-could-be-undervalued-on-its-7x-five-year-ru","date":"2026-11-18","timestamp":1795004038.155,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.912,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Can Vistra Corp (NYSE:VST) Strengthen Its S&P 500 Position?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/us/stocks/utility/can-vistra-corp-nysevst-strengthen-its-sp-500-position","date":"2026-11-18","timestamp":1795003380.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.807,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Only 11% of the Power Plants Vistra Just Bought Are in Texas. Here's Why That Matters After the Election.","source":"aol.com","link":"https://www.aol.com/articl